{"@context":["https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/ns/content/v1.jsonld",{"@base":"https://litlfred.github.io/folio/"}],"@id":"papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/rem-domain-weight-vsepr-angles","@type":["folio-assistant-core:Remark","doco:Section"],"label":"rem:domain-weight-vsepr-angles","kind":"remark","title":"Bond angles from the $q^{\\ell}$ domain weight","uses":["papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/def-h2o-molecular-braid","papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/prop-h2o-bond-angle-shift"],"interprets":"papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/prop-symmetric-block-domain-weight","tags":["VSEPR","bond-angle","AXE","hybridisation","H2S","molecular-geometry"],"text":"domain-weight-vsepr-angles.md","provenance":"authored","html":"<a id=\"rem:domain-weight-vsepr-angles\"></a><h2>Molecular consequence</h2>\n<p>Every electron domain is a braid word weighted <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">q^{\\ell}</code> on the symmetric block. A single bond <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\sigma</code> (<code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\ell{=}1</code>) weighs <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">q</code>, a double bond <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\sigma^2</code> (<code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\ell{=}2</code>) weighs <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">q^2</code>, and a lone pair <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">e^- = \\sigma_0\\sigma_1\\sigma_0</code> (<code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\ell{=}3</code>) weighs <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">q^3</code>. There is one factor of <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">q</code> per bond order, and the lone-to-bond ratio is <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">q^3/q = q^2</code>. The angle then needs only the <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">AX_nE_m</code> domain counts and the hybridisation base <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\theta_0</code>. We determine <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\theta_0</code> by whether the central atom's self-dual <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">s</code>-channel is in the bonding active set, which the atom's size (its <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">s</code>–<code class=\"language-math math-inline\">p</code> promotion gap) controls. We fit no angle.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Molecule</th>\n<th>Atom <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">(n)</code></th>\n<th><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">AX_nE_m</code></th>\n<th>Bonds</th>\n<th>Lones</th>\n<th>Base <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\theta_0</code></th>\n<th>Predicted</th>\n<th>Observed</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\mathrm{CH_4}</code></td>\n<td>C <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">(2)</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">AX_4E_0</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">4\\,\\sigma\\,(q)</code></td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>sp<code class=\"language-math math-inline\">^3</code> <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">109.47^\\circ</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">109.47\\;\\text{deg}^\\circ</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">109.5^\\circ</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\mathrm{NH_3}</code></td>\n<td>N <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">(2)</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">AX_3E_1</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">3\\,\\sigma\\,(q)</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">e^-\\,(q^3)</code></td>\n<td>sp<code class=\"language-math math-inline\">^3</code> <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">109.47^\\circ</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">106.88\\;\\text{deg}^\\circ</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">107.0^\\circ</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\mathrm{H_2O}</code></td>\n<td>O <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">(2)</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">AX_2E_2</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">2\\,\\sigma\\,(q)</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">2\\,e^-\\,(q^3)</code></td>\n<td>sp<code class=\"language-math math-inline\">^3</code> <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">109.47^\\circ</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">104.72\\;\\text{deg}^\\circ</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">104.5^\\circ</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\mathrm{C_2H_4}</code></td>\n<td>C <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">(2)</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">AX_3</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\sigma^2\\,(q^2){+}2\\,\\sigma\\,(q)</code></td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>sp<code class=\"language-math math-inline\">^2</code> <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">120^\\circ</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">117.23\\;\\text{deg}^\\circ</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">116.6^\\circ</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\mathrm{CO_2}</code></td>\n<td>C <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">(2)</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">AX_2</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">2\\,\\sigma^2\\,(q^2)</code></td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>sp <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">180^\\circ</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">180\\;\\text{deg}^\\circ</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">180.0^\\circ</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\mathrm{H_2S}</code></td>\n<td>S <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">(3)</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">AX_2E_2</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">2\\,\\sigma\\,(q)</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">2\\,e^-\\,(q^3)</code></td>\n<td>pure-<code class=\"language-math math-inline\">p</code> <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">90^\\circ</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">90\\;\\text{deg}^\\circ</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">92.1^\\circ</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The second-row atoms (C, N, O) have a small <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">s</code>–<code class=\"language-math math-inline\">p</code> gap. They hybridise, so the <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">s</code>-channel is active and the domains take the sp/sp<code class=\"language-math math-inline\">^2</code>/sp<code class=\"language-math math-inline\">^3</code> base by steric number (<code class=\"language-math math-inline\">180/120/109.47^\\circ</code>). The <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">q^3</code> lone or <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">q^2</code> double-bond weight then shifts this base. This reproduces the angles for <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\mathrm{CH_4}, \\mathrm{NH_3}, \\mathrm{H_2O}, \\mathrm{C_2H_4}, \\mathrm{CO_2}</code> within <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">0.6^\\circ</code> and specialises <a href=\"#prop:h2o-bond-angle-shift\"><code>prop:h2o-bond-angle-shift</code></a>.</p>\n<p>Third-row sulfur has a large <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">s</code>–<code class=\"language-math math-inline\">p</code> gap and does not hybridise. The <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">s</code>-channel drops from the active set, so the two bonds use the orthogonal pure-<code class=\"language-math math-inline\">p</code> orbitals with base <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\theta_0 = 90^\\circ</code>. The residual <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">+2.1^\\circ</code> (<code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\mathrm{H_2S} \\approx 92^\\circ</code>) is a lone-pair braid asymmetry that <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\mathrm{H_2O}</code> lacks. With the <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">s</code>-channel inert, the two lone pairs are inequivalent — the self-dual <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">3s^2</code> (the <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">e^-</code> palindrome, defect <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">0</code>, <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\ell{=}0</code>) and the chiral <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">3p_z</code> (non-palindromic, defect <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">2\\ell{=}2</code>). The chiral pair's directional bias opens the orbital-locked <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">90^\\circ</code>. <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\mathrm{H_2O}</code>'s two lone pairs are equal self-dual <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">e^-</code> (defects <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">0,0</code>), so it has no such tilt. We derive the sp<code class=\"language-math math-inline\">^3 \\leftrightarrow</code> pure-<code class=\"language-math math-inline\">p</code> flip from the shell braid. We use the self-dual-<code class=\"language-math math-inline\">s</code> versus chiral-<code class=\"language-math math-inline\">p</code> split plus the <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">ns</code> penetration of the <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">(n{-}1)</code> inner shells, <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\Delta(n)\\propto(n{-}1) = \\text{central-atom size}</code>. This produces a discrete flip at <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">n{=}2{\\to}3</code> with no fit. The group-16 sequence <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\mathrm{O}\\,104.5^\\circ \\to \\mathrm{S/Se/Te}\\sim 90^\\circ</code> confirms the saturation.</p>\n","source":"folio/quantum-observable-universe/molecular-construction/domain-weight-vsepr-angles.md","heading":"Remark","number":"17.8.7"}
